{"post_stream":{"posts":[{"id":303305,"name":"Ethan Furman","username":"stoneleaf","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/stoneleaf/{size}/88_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-08T22:56:07.596Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eContinuing the discussion from \u003ca href=\"https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns/109100\"\u003ePEP 845: Leading-Dot Value Patterns\u003c/a\u003e:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003caside class=\"quote no-group quote-modified\" data-username=\"tmk\" data-display-name=\"Thomas Kehrenberg\" data-post=\"1\" data-topic=\"109100\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/tmk/48/10935_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns/109100/1\"\u003ePEP 845: Leading-Dot Value Patterns\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor your consideration: \u003ca href=\"https://peps.python.org/pep-0845/\"\u003ePEP 845\u003c/a\u003e, with the following abstract:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis PEP enables the use of non-attribute names as \u003cstrong\u003evalue patterns\u003c/strong\u003e in match statements. A name prefixed with a dot is looked up using the normal name resolution rules and compared to the match subject by equality in the same manner as existing value patterns, instead of being treated as a \u003cstrong\u003ecapture pattern\u003c/strong\u003e. This makes it possible to match local variables and global variables defined in the same module without using a guard clause.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003edef get_book_titles_for_author(author: str):\n    for book in BOOKS:\n        match book:\n            case {\"title\": title, \"author\": .author}:\n                yield title\n\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can also try it out in the demo here: \u003ca href=\"https://pep845-demo.pages.dev/\"\u003ehttps://pep845-demo.pages.dev/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://peps.python.org/pep-0845/\" class=\"inline-onebox\"\u003ePEP 845 – Leading-Dot Value Patterns | peps.python.org\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePlease chime in with your thoughts!\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":1,"post_type":1,"posts_count":16,"updated_at":"2026-10-08T22:56:07.596Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":20,"reads":211,"readers_count":210,"score":196.4,"yours":false,"topic_id":109405,"topic_slug":"pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement","display_username":"Ethan Furman","primary_group_name":"committers","flair_name":"committers","flair_url":"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/uploads/python1/original/1X/ca0883b21b8210c5a62c9ac02353de5f24a4d6fc.png","flair_bg_color":"006b94","flair_color":"fac731","flair_group_id":41,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns/109100","internal":true,"reflection":false,"title":"PEP 845: Leading-Dot Value Patterns","clicks":56},{"url":"https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns/109100/11","internal":true,"reflection":true,"title":"PEP 845: Leading-Dot Value Patterns","clicks":1}],"read":true,"user_title":"CPython core developer","title_is_group":true,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":4}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":82,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement/109405/1","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":4}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":4,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null,"can_vote":false},{"id":303320,"name":"Steve Dower","username":"steve.dower","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/steve.dower/{size}/56_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-09T10:06:35.756Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eI assume you’ve done lots of bikeshedding on the choice of punctuation already, so all I’ll say is that I would never guess that’s what the \u003ccode\u003e.\u003c/code\u003e meant without reading the definition (multiple times), and \u003cem\u003ewould confidently guess it did something different\u003c/em\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eVirtually any other symbol would give me pause, rather than assuming wrong. Even a double dot would throw me enough to go “I need to look this up”.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA single dot with no context implies to me that the context is thing we’re matching on, rather than locals.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":2,"post_type":1,"posts_count":16,"updated_at":"2026-10-09T10:06:35.756Z","reply_count":2,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":183,"readers_count":182,"score":126.2,"yours":false,"topic_id":109405,"topic_slug":"pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement","display_username":"Steve Dower","primary_group_name":"committers","flair_name":"committers","flair_url":"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/uploads/python1/original/1X/ca0883b21b8210c5a62c9ac02353de5f24a4d6fc.png","flair_bg_color":"006b94","flair_color":"fac731","flair_group_id":41,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"CPython core developer","title_is_group":true,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":5}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":54,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement/109405/2","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":5}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":5,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":303343,"name":"Matthew","username":"matthewyu0311","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/matthewyu0311/{size}/20118_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-09T13:33:19.552Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eReading the original PEP and discussion again it seems what has not really been discussed is the trailing dot. But I can assume that something like \u003ccode\u003ecase foo.:\u003c/code\u003e would be probably harder to read than the leading dot.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnd I suppose that matching to an \u003cem\u003eattribute of the thing itself being matched\u003c/em\u003e is a rarer use case that can already be addressed by pulling the entire thing to be matched out to a local variable , and then doing a plain old attribute lookup pattern on it.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":3,"post_type":1,"posts_count":16,"updated_at":"2026-10-09T13:33:19.552Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":2,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":171,"readers_count":170,"score":38.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":109405,"topic_slug":"pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement","display_username":"Matthew","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"reply_to_user":{"id":54,"username":"steve.dower","name":"Steve Dower","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/steve.dower/{size}/56_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":31222,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement/109405/3","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":303352,"name":"Ethan Furman","username":"stoneleaf","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/stoneleaf/{size}/88_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-09T14:52:53.820Z","cooked":"\u003caside class=\"quote group-committers quote-modified\" data-username=\"steve.dower\" data-display-name=\"Steve Dower\" data-post=\"2\" data-topic=\"109405\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/steve.dower/48/56_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e Steve Dower:\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eand \u003cem\u003ewould confidently guess [the leading dot] did something different\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI’m curious, what would you guess it did?\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":4,"post_type":1,"posts_count":16,"updated_at":"2026-10-09T14:52:53.820Z","reply_count":2,"reply_to_post_number":2,"quote_count":1,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":162,"readers_count":161,"score":47.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":109405,"topic_slug":"pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement","display_username":"Ethan Furman","primary_group_name":"committers","flair_name":"committers","flair_url":"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/uploads/python1/original/1X/ca0883b21b8210c5a62c9ac02353de5f24a4d6fc.png","flair_bg_color":"006b94","flair_color":"fac731","flair_group_id":41,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"CPython core developer","title_is_group":true,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":82,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement/109405/4","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":303353,"name":"Steve Dower","username":"steve.dower","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/steve.dower/{size}/56_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-09T14:56:57.668Z","cooked":"\u003caside class=\"quote group-committers\" data-username=\"stoneleaf\" data-display-name=\"Ethan Furman\" data-post=\"4\" data-topic=\"109405\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/stoneleaf/48/88_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e Ethan Furman:\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ewhat would you guess it did?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSomething along the lines of \u003ccode\u003ebook.author\u003c/code\u003e, and then I’d have to figure out what that would actually mean as a match constraint. One of those things that at first glance seems reasonable, and very quickly appears to be nonsense, but now I have to unwind a whole lot of thinking to go “oh, it actually means \u003ccode\u003e==author\u003c/code\u003e”.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":5,"post_type":1,"posts_count":16,"updated_at":"2026-10-09T14:56:57.668Z","reply_count":3,"reply_to_post_number":4,"quote_count":1,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":157,"readers_count":156,"score":121.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":109405,"topic_slug":"pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement","display_username":"Steve Dower","primary_group_name":"committers","flair_name":"committers","flair_url":"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/uploads/python1/original/1X/ca0883b21b8210c5a62c9ac02353de5f24a4d6fc.png","flair_bg_color":"006b94","flair_color":"fac731","flair_group_id":41,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"CPython core developer","title_is_group":true,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":5}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":54,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement/109405/5","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":5}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":5,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":303354,"name":"layday","username":"layday","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/layday/{size}/2771_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-09T15:00:12.523Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eWhy not make it so you have to specify the target when matching against a variable?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003edef get_book_titles_for_author(author: str):\n    for book in BOOKS:\n        match book:\n            case {\"title\": title, \"author\": author as author}:\n                yield title\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis is currently a syntax error, so it’s up for grabs.  I feel this is (perhaps slightly) better than inventing another meaning for yet another Python symbol.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eEdit: Ah, but of course, \u003ccode\u003ea as b\u003c/code\u003e is valid today and assigns the value to both \u003ccode\u003ea\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003eb\u003c/code\u003e, so there’s no hope of detecting typos.  I don’t know what the usefulness of \u003ccode\u003ea as b\u003c/code\u003e might be, so perhaps it should be deprecated regardless.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":6,"post_type":1,"posts_count":16,"updated_at":"2026-10-09T15:08:01.355Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":154,"readers_count":153,"score":30.4,"yours":false,"topic_id":109405,"topic_slug":"pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement","display_username":"layday","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":2,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":1424,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement/109405/6","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":303356,"name":"Arthur Neufeld","username":"AJNeufeld","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/ajneufeld/{size}/12174_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-09T15:23:19.098Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eThe issue is interpreting \u003ccode\u003eauthor\u003c/code\u003e as a value instead of \u003ccode\u003eauthor\u003c/code\u003e as an identifier, right?  Why not just convert it into a value?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003edef get_book_titles_for_author(author: str):\n    for book in BOOKS:\n        match book:\n            case {\"title\": title, \"author\": author + \"\"}:\n                yield title\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis is currently a syntax error, but I think it would be clear enough that you want the value of the expression to be used as the “value pattern”.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eObviously, for numbers you’d need to add zero, and lists you’d add a empty list, and for a tuple you’d add an empty tuple, and for …  Hmm, ok, maybe we don’t want to actually add anything; we just want it to be interpreted an expression instead of an identifier.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003edef get_book_titles_for_author(author: str):\n    for book in BOOKS:\n        match book:\n            case {\"title\": title, \"author\": (author)}:\n                yield title\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThere we go; it is an expression!\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":7,"post_type":1,"posts_count":16,"updated_at":"2026-10-09T15:23:19.098Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":152,"readers_count":151,"score":70.2,"yours":false,"topic_id":109405,"topic_slug":"pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement","display_username":"Arthur Neufeld","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":2}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":11643,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement/109405/7","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":2}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":2,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":303363,"name":"A Reich","username":"a-reich","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/a-reich/{size}/5885_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-09T16:07:28.903Z","cooked":"\u003caside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"AJNeufeld\" data-display-name=\"Arthur Neufeld\" data-post=\"7\" data-topic=\"109405\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/ajneufeld/48/12174_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e Arthur Neufeld:\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003edef get_book_titles_for_author(author: str):\n    for book in BOOKS:\n        match book:\n            case {\"title\": title, \"author\": (author)}:\n                yield title\n\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThere we go; it is an expression!\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis syntax with \u003ccode\u003e(author)\u003c/code\u003e is already valid and interpreted the same as the bare name, so that would be a backwards incompatible change.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":9,"post_type":1,"posts_count":16,"updated_at":"2026-10-09T16:07:28.903Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":7,"quote_count":1,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":146,"readers_count":145,"score":34.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":109405,"topic_slug":"pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement","display_username":"A Reich","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":6098,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement/109405/9","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":303372,"name":"Arthur Neufeld","username":"AJNeufeld","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/ajneufeld/{size}/12174_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-09T19:02:01.816Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eAnd \u003ccode\u003estr(author)\u003c/code\u003e doesn’t work, 'cause that is just another deconstruction pattern that binds a string to \u003ccode\u003eauthor\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":10,"post_type":1,"posts_count":16,"updated_at":"2026-10-09T19:02:01.816Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":9,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":137,"readers_count":136,"score":32.2,"yours":false,"topic_id":109405,"topic_slug":"pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement","display_username":"Arthur Neufeld","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"reply_to_user":{"id":6098,"username":"a-reich","name":"A Reich","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/a-reich/{size}/5885_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":11643,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement/109405/10","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":303377,"name":"Carol Willing","username":"willingc","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/willingc/{size}/20_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-09T20:56:43.579Z","cooked":"\u003caside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"tmk\" data-post=\"1\" data-topic=\"109100\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/tmk/48/10935_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns/109100/1\"\u003ePEP 845: Leading-Dot Value Patterns\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis PEP enables the use of non-attribute names as \u003cstrong\u003evalue patterns\u003c/strong\u003e in match statements. A name prefixed with a dot is looked up using the normal name resolution rules and compared to the match subject by equality in the same manner as existing value patterns, instead of being treated as a \u003cstrong\u003ecapture pattern\u003c/strong\u003e. This makes it possible to match local variables and global variables defined in the same module without using a guard clause.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca class=\"mention\" href=\"/u/stoneleaf\"\u003e@stoneleaf\u003c/a\u003e I felt similarly to \u003ca class=\"mention\" href=\"/u/steve.dower\"\u003e@steve.dower\u003c/a\u003e. I had to reread the PEP abstract several times to tease out the meaning. This sentence “This makes it possible to match local variables and global variables defined in the same module without using a guard clause.” seems to me to be the compelling reason for the PEP.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe “Leading-Dot” in the PEP name adds more confusion to me than the desire to match without using a guard. It may be helpful to reword the abstract and perhaps add a second example to illustrate the global and local value match with the guard (current) and without the guard (this PEP).\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":11,"post_type":1,"posts_count":16,"updated_at":"2026-10-09T20:56:43.579Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":5,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":2,"reads":136,"readers_count":135,"score":37.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":109405,"topic_slug":"pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement","display_username":"Carol Willing","primary_group_name":"committers","flair_name":"committers","flair_url":"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/uploads/python1/original/1X/ca0883b21b8210c5a62c9ac02353de5f24a4d6fc.png","flair_bg_color":"006b94","flair_color":"fac731","flair_group_id":41,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns/109100","internal":true,"reflection":false,"title":"PEP 845: Leading-Dot Value Patterns","clicks":0}],"read":true,"user_title":"CPython core developer","title_is_group":true,"reply_to_user":{"id":54,"username":"steve.dower","name":"Steve Dower","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/steve.dower/{size}/56_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":14,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement/109405/11","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":303401,"name":"Jelle Zijlstra","username":"Jelle","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/jelle/{size}/1049_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-10T04:07:27.967Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003e==\u003c/code\u003e might actually be a good syntax for this.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003edef get_book_titles_for_author(author: str):\n    for book in BOOKS:\n        match book:\n            case {\"title\": title, \"author\": ==author}:\n                yield title\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e(You could even extend it with similar syntax for other compare ops like \u003ccode\u003e!=\u003c/code\u003e, though I’d struggle to see the use.)\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":12,"post_type":1,"posts_count":16,"updated_at":"2026-10-10T04:07:27.967Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":5,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":126,"readers_count":125,"score":140.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":109405,"topic_slug":"pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement","display_username":"Jelle Zijlstra","primary_group_name":"committers","flair_name":"committers","flair_url":"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/uploads/python1/original/1X/ca0883b21b8210c5a62c9ac02353de5f24a4d6fc.png","flair_bg_color":"006b94","flair_color":"fac731","flair_group_id":41,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"CPython core developer","title_is_group":true,"reply_to_user":{"id":54,"username":"steve.dower","name":"Steve Dower","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/steve.dower/{size}/56_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":7}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":9,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement/109405/12","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":6},{"id":"+1","type":"emoji","count":1}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":7,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":303409,"name":"Matthew","username":"matthewyu0311","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/matthewyu0311/{size}/20118_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-10T09:27:57.169Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eBesides having to use guard clauses there’s also another workaround. The standard library currently provides no way for programmatic chained local+nonlocal+global+builtin name lookup, but this can be emulated:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003elazy import inspect\nclass VariableLookup:\n    \"\"\"Provides dot lookup for local, nonlocal, global variables and builtins.\n    Relies on interpreter live stack frame support. \n    \"\"\"\n    __slots__ = ()\n    def __getattr__(self, name: str, /):\n        frame = inspect.currentframe()\n        depth = 0\n        while frame is not None:\n            if depth \u0026gt; 0:\n                local = frame.f_locals\n                try:\n                    return local[name]\n                except KeyError:\n                    # We encounter the module frame, stop here\n                    if local == frame.f_globals:\n                        try:\n                            return frame.f_builtins[name]\n                        except KeyError:\n                            raise NameError(f\"name {name!r} is not defined\", name=name) from None\n            depth += 1\n            frame = frame.f_back\n        # Either there's no frame support from the interpreter \n        # or we got incomplete frames\n        raise NotImplementedError(\"no or incomplete stack frame support\")\n\nVariables = VariableLookup()\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIf only locals and globals are concerned, one can use \u003ccode\u003eVariables = types.SimpleNamespace(globals() | locals())\u003c/code\u003e at each match site.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThen we can simply use the existing value lookup pattern:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003ea = \"a\"\nb = \"b\"\ndef fn(foo):\n    match foo:\n        case Variables.a:\n            print(\"matches a\")\n        case Variables.b:\n            print(\"matches b\")\nfn(\"a\") # matches a\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIf variable value patterns (leading dot or otherwise) are not deemed useful enough to warrant syntax changes (40% of respondents surveyed in the PEP were fine with the status quo) I think the above (obviously will need to be implemented in C) will be the next best option. Granted, these are not the best-looking workarounds, but I don’t think \u003ca href=\"https://peps.python.org/pep-0845/#scope-qualified-lookups-global-name-nonlocal-name\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003eScope-qualified lookups\u003c/a\u003e should be completely rejected off-hand.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":13,"post_type":1,"posts_count":16,"updated_at":"2026-10-10T09:27:57.169Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":123,"readers_count":122,"score":59.4,"yours":false,"topic_id":109405,"topic_slug":"pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement","display_username":"Matthew","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://peps.python.org/pep-0845/#scope-qualified-lookups-global-name-nonlocal-name","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"PEP 845 – Leading-Dot Value Patterns | peps.python.org","clicks":1}],"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":2}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":31222,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement/109405/13","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":2}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":2,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":303410,"name":"Anton Agestam","username":"antonagestam","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/antonagestam/{size}/12473_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-10T10:32:27.235Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eThis looks OK in a dict pattern, but I wonder would this would look like in an object pattern, would it result in triple equal sign then? Or maybe it collapses to a double one there as well.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003ecase Book(author===author):\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003evs\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003ecase Book(author==author):\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI could see valid arguments for both, but I think the underlying question is if this should be thought of as a prefix operator or not. Would it be valid to do this?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003ecase Book(author=(==author)):\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e","post_number":14,"post_type":1,"posts_count":16,"updated_at":"2026-10-10T10:32:27.235Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":12,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":119,"readers_count":118,"score":93.6,"yours":false,"topic_id":109405,"topic_slug":"pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement","display_username":"Anton Agestam","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"reply_to_user":{"id":9,"username":"Jelle","name":"Jelle Zijlstra","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/jelle/{size}/1049_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":4}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":11933,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement/109405/14","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":3},{"id":"open_mouth","type":"emoji","count":1}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":4,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":303427,"name":"Jelle Zijlstra","username":"Jelle","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/jelle/{size}/1049_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-10T17:06:11.042Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eGood point. \u003ccode\u003ecase Book(author===author):\u003c/code\u003e would be the logically consistent answer, but (1) that looks terrible and (2) it tokenizes as \u003ccode\u003e==\u003c/code\u003e followed by \u003ccode\u003e=\u003c/code\u003e, not the reverse. Parenthesizing it with \u003ccode\u003ecase Book(author=(==author))\u003c/code\u003e seems like the best solution, but I’d definitely count this as an argument against the \u003ccode\u003e==\u003c/code\u003e syntax.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":15,"post_type":1,"posts_count":16,"updated_at":"2026-10-10T17:06:11.042Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":14,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":112,"readers_count":111,"score":72.2,"yours":false,"topic_id":109405,"topic_slug":"pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement","display_username":"Jelle Zijlstra","primary_group_name":"committers","flair_name":"committers","flair_url":"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/uploads/python1/original/1X/ca0883b21b8210c5a62c9ac02353de5f24a4d6fc.png","flair_bg_color":"006b94","flair_color":"fac731","flair_group_id":41,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"CPython core developer","title_is_group":true,"reply_to_user":{"id":11933,"username":"antonagestam","name":"Anton Agestam","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/antonagestam/{size}/12473_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":3}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":9,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement/109405/15","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":3}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":3,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":303513,"name":"Ethan Furman","username":"stoneleaf","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/stoneleaf/{size}/88_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-12T18:22:47.522Z","cooked":"\u003caside class=\"quote group-committers quote-modified\" data-username=\"steve.dower\" data-display-name=\"Steve Dower\" data-post=\"5\" data-topic=\"109405\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/steve.dower/48/56_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e Steve Dower:\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003caside class=\"quote group-committers\" data-username=\"stoneleaf\" data-display-name=\"Ethan Furman\" data-post=\"4\" data-topic=\"109405\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/stoneleaf/48/88_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e Ethan Furman:\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ewhat would you guess it did?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSomething along the lines of \u003ccode\u003ebook.author\u003c/code\u003e, and then I’d have to figure out […]\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI sympathize.  It took a while for me to internalize that Python’s \u003ccode\u003ewith\u003c/code\u003e was nothing like Foxpro’s \u003ccode\u003ewith\u003c/code\u003e.  Considering the use of the dot in match statements currently, I would hope that the leading dot would be a gentle learning curve.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":16,"post_type":1,"posts_count":16,"updated_at":"2026-10-12T18:22:47.522Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":5,"quote_count":2,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":83,"readers_count":82,"score":21.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":109405,"topic_slug":"pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement","display_username":"Ethan Furman","primary_group_name":"committers","flair_name":"committers","flair_url":"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/uploads/python1/original/1X/ca0883b21b8210c5a62c9ac02353de5f24a4d6fc.png","flair_bg_color":"006b94","flair_color":"fac731","flair_group_id":41,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"CPython core developer","title_is_group":true,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":82,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement/109405/16","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":303519,"name":"Jamie Gravendeel","username":"monster","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/monster/{size}/36312_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-12T19:34:23.717Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eI’m personally dubious of syntax proposals that don’t use a dot, considering matching with variables in namespaces requires no extra syntax.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eStructural pattern matching is also quite intricate, and users will have to learn its syntax by reading the documentation either way. Considering this, I think users would understand the leading dot syntax quite well in context.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn any case, I’m very happy to see this PEP since variable matching always felt like an afterthought to me while using structural pattern matching.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":17,"post_type":1,"posts_count":16,"updated_at":"2026-10-12T19:34:23.717Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":81,"readers_count":80,"score":35.4,"yours":false,"topic_id":109405,"topic_slug":"pep-845-leading-dot-value-patterns-for-match-statement","display_user